## Metal–Organic Frameworks
Chemical and Structural Stability of Zirconium-based Metal–Organic Frameworks with Large Three-Dimensional Pores by Linker Engineering
Two linkers of equivalent connectivity but quite distinct torsional flexibility are used to produce \([Z\mathsf{r}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{4}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}(\mathsf{L}\mathsf{T})_{2.6}(\mathsf{L}\mathsf{2})_{0.4}]\) - (solvent), that combines both structural and chemical stability in a metal–organic
framework. Such a combination is not accessible for the single-linker isostructural phases. The resulting Zr-based MOF has three-dimensional channels and a high surface area of \(4184\;\mathsf{m}^{2}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) .
